<article class="env-corollary" id="06ME">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06ME">Corollary <span data-tag="06ME">7.3.1.15</span>.</a> Let $G: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories, let $K$ be a simplicial set, and let $\alpha : F_0 \rightarrow F_1$ be an isomorphism between diagrams $F_0, F_1: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Suppose we are given another diagram $H: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ and a natural transformation $\beta : G \circ F_1 \rightarrow H$. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The natural transformation $\beta $ exhibits $G$ as a right Kan extension of $H$ along $F_1$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The composite natural transformation $G \circ F_0 \xrightarrow { \alpha } G \circ F_1 \xrightarrow {\beta } H$ exhibits $G$ as a right Kan extension of $H$ along $F_0$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Using Corollary <a data-tag="00UW" href="/tag/00UW">3.1.8.2</a>, we can choose a monomorphism $\iota : \Delta ^1 \rightarrow Q$, where $Q$ is a contractible Kan complex. Let us identify $\iota $ with an edge $e: q_0 \rightarrow q_1$ of $Q$. Since $\iota $ is a weak homotopy equivalence, there is a morphism of simplicial sets $f: Q \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq }$ satisfying $t(e) = \alpha $. Let us identify $f$ with a diagram $F: Q \times K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Let $\widetilde{H}$ denote the composite map $Q \times K \rightarrow K \xrightarrow {H} \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. Since the inclusion $\{  q_1 \}  \times K \hookrightarrow Q \times K$ is a categorical equivalence, we can extend $\alpha $ to a natural transformation $\widetilde{\alpha }: G \circ F \rightarrow \widetilde{H}$. Unwinding the definitions, we see that the composite natural transformation $G \circ F_0 \xrightarrow { \alpha } G \circ F_1 \xrightarrow {\beta } H$ can be identified with the restriction of $\widetilde{\alpha }$ to $\{  q_0 \}  \times K$. Applying Proposition <a data-tag="02YL" href="/tag/02YL">7.3.1.14</a> twice, conclude that $(a)$ and $(b)$ are both equivalent to the requirement that $\widetilde{\alpha }$ exhibits $G$ as a right Kan extension of $\widetilde{H}$ along $F$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
